// error
// #2236, the float arm of §17.4's result rule — kept as its own case because it
// is the one where the returned value never goes near the register the exit
// status is read from. `Op.Ret` picks its register class from its argument's
// type, so an `f64` result leaves d0/xmm0 while `boot` reads x0/eax: measured
// before the rule, `function main(): f64 { print("hello\n"); return 1.5 }`
// printed `hello` and exited **6** — a number with no relationship to 1.5, to
// success, or to anything else.
//
// `bool` is the same hole with a more plausible-looking answer (`return true`
// exited 1, i.e. a successful program reporting failure); it needs no separate
// case because both go through the one `vEntryResult` predicate, and this is the
// arm where the value travels in the wrong register file entirely.
function main(): f64 {
  print("hello\n")
  return 1.5
}
